        "math_academy_id": "q-64021",
        "problem": "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $x-y<3?$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & <3 \\\\ -y & <-x+3 \\\\ y & >x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=x-3.\n$$\n\n Since the inequality involves a 'strictly greater than' sign, the line $y=x-3$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a **dashed line**.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny>x-3\n$$\n\n refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $y=x-3,$ so we shade *above* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/77a4c9b6bc705a2fbf486bfef5a6ce57924aae86c4abf1510040c7957b873329.png)",
